.TH SDL_CreateGPUShader 3 "SDL 3.5.0" "Simple Directmedia Layer" "SDL3 FUNCTIONS"
.SH NAME
SDL_CreateGPUShader \- Creates a shader to be used when creating a graphics pipeline.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <SDL3/SDL_gpu.h>
.PP
.BI "SDL_GPUShader * SDL_CreateGPUShader(
.BI "    SDL_GPUDevice *device,
.BI "    const SDL_GPUShaderCreateInfo *createinfo);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Shader resource bindings must be authored to follow a particular convention depending on the shader format\[char46] See below for details\[char46]
.PP
---
.PP
.BR "SPIR-V (GLSL)"
.PP
For vertex shaders, use: - Set 0 for samplers, storage textures, and storage buffers - Set 1 for uniform data
.PP
For fragment shaders, use: - Set 2 for samplers, storage textures, and storage buffers - Set 3 for uniform data
.PP
The first resource in a given set must have a
.BR binding
of 0\[char46] Additional resources must appear at consecutive bindings (1, 2, etc), leaving no gaps in the set\[char46]
.PP
All samplers must come first in the binding order, in order of how they are bound via
.BR SDL_BindGPU*Samplers() \[char46]
.PP
All storage textures must come after all samplers in the binding order, in order of how they are bound via
.BR SDL_Bind*StorageTextures() \[char46]
.PP
All storage buffers must come after all storage textures in the binding order, in order of how they are bound via
.BR SDL_Bind*StorageBuffers() \[char46]
.PP
.BR Example
.PP
If a vertex shader binds 2 samplers, 2 storage textures, 2 storage buffers, and 2 uniform buffers, its binding layout should look like this:

.IP
.EX
// Any samplers come first in the set, in SDL bind slot order
layout(set = 0, binding = 0) uniform sampler2D samplerBoundToSlot0;
layout(set = 0, binding = 1) uniform sampler2D samplerBoundToSlot1;
// Any storage textures come next in the set, in SDL bind slot order
layout(set = 0, binding = 2) uniform image2D storageTextureBoundToSlot0;
layout(set = 0, binding = 3) uniform image2D storageTextureBoundToSlot1;
// Any storage buffers come next in the set, in SDL bind slot order
layout(set = 0, binding = 4) buffer storageBufferBoundToSlot0 { ... };
layout(set = 0, binding = 5) buffer storageBufferBoundToSlot1 { ... };
// Any uniform buffers are in their own set, in SDL slot order
layout(set = 1, binding = 0) uniform UniformDataBoundToSlot0 { ... };
layout(set = 1, binding = 1) uniform UniformDataBoundToSlot1 { ... };
.EE
.PP

---
.PP
.BR "DXBC / DXIL (HLSL)"
.PP
For vertex shaders, use: -
.BR "(t[n], space0)"
for sampled textures, storage textures, and storage buffers -
.BR "(s[n], space0)"
for samplers -
.BR "(b[n], space1)"
for uniform data
.PP
For fragment (aka \(dqpixel\(dq) shaders, use: -
.BR "(t[n], space2)"
for sampled textures, storage textures, and storage buffers -
.BR "(s[n], space2)"
for samplers -
.BR "(b[n], space3)"
for uniform data
.PP
The first resource in a given register set must have a register index of
.BR 0 \[char46]
Additional resources must appear at consecutive indices (1, 2, etc), leaving no gaps in the register set\[char46]
.PP
All sampled textures must come first in the
.BR t
register set, in order of how they are bound via
.BR SDL_BindGPU*Samplers() \[char46]
.PP
All sampler objects must be in the
.BR s
register set, in the same order as the textures above\[char46]
.PP
All storage textures must come after all samplers in the
.BR t
register set, in order of how they are bound via
.BR SDL_Bind*StorageTextures() \[char46]
.PP
All storage buffers must come after all storage textures in the
.BR t
register set, in order of how they are bound via
.BR SDL_Bind*StorageBuffers() \[char46]
.PP
.BR Example
.PP
If a pixel shader binds 2 samplers, 2 storage textures, 2 storage buffers, and 2 uniform buffers, its binding layout should look like this:

.IP
.EX
// Any samplers and sampled textures come first in their respective register sets, in SDL bind slot order
SamplerState SamplerBoundToSlot0 : register( s0, space2 );
SamplerState SamplerBoundToSlot1 : register( s1, space2 );
Texture2D SampledTextureBoundToSlot0 : register( t0, space2 );
Texture2D SampledTextureBoundToSlot1 : register( t1, space2 );
// Any storage textures come next in the `t` register set, in SDL bind slot order
Texture2D StorageTextureBoundToSlot0 : register( t2, space2 );
Texture2D StorageTextureBoundToSlot1 : register( t3, space2 );
// Any storage buffers come next in the `t` register set, in SDL bind slot order
ByteAddressBuffer StorageBufferBoundToSlot0 : register( t4, space2 );
ByteAddressBuffer StorageBufferBoundToSlot1 : register( t5, space2 );
// Any uniform buffers are in the `b` register set *and* in their own space, in SDL slot order
cbuffer UniformDataBoundToSlot0 : register( b0, space3 ) { ... };
cbuffer UniformDataBoundToSlot1 : register( b1, space3 ) { ... };
.EE
.PP

---
.PP
.BR "MSL / Metallib (Metal Shading Language)"
.PP
The first resource in a given argument table must have an index of
.BR 0 \[char46]
Additional resources must appear at consecutive indices (1, 2, etc), leaving no gaps in the table\[char46] (_Except_ in the case of vertex buffers, which are mentioned below\[char46])
.PP
All sampled textures must come first in the
.BR [[texture]]
argument table, in order of how they are bound via
.BR SDL_BindGPU*Samplers() \[char46]
.PP
All sampler objects must be in the
.BR [[sampler]]
argument table, in the same order as the textures above\[char46]
.PP
All storage textures must come after all sampled textures in the
.BR [[texture]]
argument table, in order of how they are bound via
.BR SDL_BindGPU*StorageTextures() \[char46]
.PP
All uniform buffers must come first in the
.BR [[buffer]]
argument table, in order of their slots in
.BR SDL_PushGPU*UniformData() \[char46]
.PP
All storage buffers must come after all uniform buffers in the
.BR [[buffer]]
argument table, in order of how they are bound via
.BR SDL_BindGPU*StorageBuffers() \[char46]
.PP
In Metal, vertex buffers are also included in the
.BR [[buffer]]
argument table\[char46] To work around this, SDL forces the vertex buffer bound to slot 0 to be bound at
.BR [[buffer(14)]] \[char46]
The vertex buffer in slot 1 will be bound to
.BR [[buffer(15)]] ,
and so on\[char46] Rather than manually authoring vertex buffer indices, use the
.BR [[stage_in]]
attribute which will automatically use the vertex input information from the
.BR SDL_GPUGraphicsPipeline \[char46]
.PP
.BR Example
.PP
For a vertex shader with 1 vertex buffer, 2 samplers, 2 storage textures, 2 storage buffers, and 2 uniform buffers, the main function signature should look something like this:

.IP
.EX
vertex VertexOutput ExampleVertexShader(
    // Vertex buffers are their own special thing...
    SomeVertexInput input [[stage_in]], // alternatively, SomeVertexInput input [[buffer(14)]]
    // Any samplers go in the `sampler` table, in SDL bind slot order
    sampler samplerBoundToSlot0 [[sampler(0)]],
    sampler samplerBoundToSlot1 [[sampler(1)]],
    // Any sampled textures come first in the `texture` table, in SDL bind slot order
    texture2d<float> sampledTextureBoundToSlot0 [[texture(0)]],
    texture2d<float> sampledTextureBoundToSlot1 [[texture(1)]],
    // Any storage textures come next in the `texture` table, in SDL bind slot order
    texture2d<float> storageTextureBoundToSlot0 [[texture(2)]],
    texture2d<float> storageTextureBoundToSlot1 [[texture(3)]],
    // Any uniform buffers come first in the `buffer` table, in SDL slot order
    constant SomeUniformStruct uniformDataBoundToSlot0 [[buffer(0)]],
    constant SomeUniformStruct uniformDataBoundToSlot1 [[buffer(1)]],
    // Any storage buffers come next in the `buffer` table, in SDL bind slot order
    device SomeBufferStruct& storageBufferBoundToSlot0 [[buffer(2)]],
    device SomeBufferStruct& storageBufferBoundToSlot1 [[buffer(3)]]);
.EE
.PP

---
.PP
Shader semantics other than system-value semantics do not matter in D3D12\[char46] For ease of use, the SDL implementation assumes that non system-value semantics will all be
.BR TEXCOORD \[char46]
If you are using HLSL as the shader source language, your vertex semantics should start at
.BR TEXCOORD0
and increment like so:
.BR TEXCOORD1 ,

.BR TEXCOORD2 ,
etc\[char46]
.PP
If you wish to change the semantic prefix to something other than
.BR TEXCOORD
you can use
.BR SDL_PROP_GPU_DEVICE_CREATE_D3D12_SEMANTIC_NAME_STRING
with
.BR SDL_CreateGPUDeviceWithProperties ()\[char46]
.PP
There are optional properties that can be provided through
.BR props \[char46]
These are the supported properties:
.PP

\(bu
.BR SDL_PROP_GPU_SHADER_CREATE_NAME_STRING :
a name that can be displayed in debugging tools\[char46]

.SH FUNCTION PARAMETERS
.TP
.I device
a GPU Context\[char46]
.TP
.I createinfo
a struct describing the state of the shader to create\[char46]
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns a shader object on success, or NULL on failure; call
.BR SDL_GetError ()
for more information\[char46]

.SH AVAILABILITY
This function is available since SDL 3\[char46]2\[char46]0\[char46]

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR SDL_CreateGPUGraphicsPipeline (3),
.BR SDL_ReleaseGPUShader (3)
